  "query_preview": "..A trough axis is taking forever to make its way \nthrough Arizona tonight, as the parent upper low meanders through \nthe northern Great Basin and eventually into Wyoming. This allowed \nfor continued moisture transport into eastern Arizona, specifically \nApache County in our CWA. Showers and thunderstorms will continue \nover this area until around daybreak, when the drier air finally \narrives.\n\nAt this point, the remainder of the weekend will be dry for all of \nthe area, as dew points drop into the lower 30s, maybe even upper \n20s. This means some some cold mornings, especially Sunday morning. \nA few pockets of freezing temperatures may develop in sheltered \nvalleys above 6500 feet.\n\nBy early next week, a new upper low arrives, this time taking a more \nsouthern route. Dynamics and renewed southerly flow will once again \nmove moisture northward, allowing for isolated to scattered showers \nand thunderstorms to form, mainly by Tuesday. This will not be a \ndeep moisture push like we saw a few days ago, but definitely enough \nto bring PoPs back into the picture.\n\nThis pattern hangs out for the remainder of the week, especially \nover the eastern zones, as a possible sub-tropical push develops.\nWe will keep seasonable temperatures and a mention of isolated to \nscattered convection in the picture a a result, basically from\nlate Monday/early Tuesday onward.",
